  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1134 PM MST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nThe first part of this storm system arrived earlier this evening\nas a band of rain and snow progressed from southwest to northeast\nacross the mountains. There was enough moisture to maintain this\nband of precipitation as far east as the continental divide, with\nwebcams from CDOT and various ski areas showing some brief light\nto moderate snow... though accumulations were generally around an\ninch. A few orographic snow showers are expected to continue\novernight with minimal additional snowfall totals.\n\nThe second part of this system will be the wind. Surface\nobservations across the higher Front Range foothills have been\nslowly increasing over the last couple of hours, with a few gusts\nin the 45-50 mph range noted in the last hour or so. Near term\nguidance has been too bullish with the initial development of the\nmountain wave, and I would expect that we continue to see a\nslow increase in wind gusts overnight before the mountain wave\nbegins to become better established closer to 12z. From an\ningredients perspective just about everything is \"marginal\". While\nthe ridgetop/cross-barrier flow is around 45-55kt, the stable\nlayer is somewhat diffuse and a bit higher than you'd expect for a\nmore classic mountain wave. The setup is also rather brief - with\nthe wave quickly diminishing after 18z Wednesday when winds \ntransition to more of a subsident/gradient type regime. Ultimately\nthe current headlines seem representative enough. We still expect\na few gusts around 75 mph over the foothills with a few gusts to \naround 60 mph possible for the typical wind prone areas near the \nbase of the foothills. These winds will also produce some fire \nweather concerns during the day (discussed further below).\n\nThursday still looks to be a quiet day across the forecast area as\nwe sit in between systems in a dry southwesterly flow aloft.\nForecast high temperatures will be back in the 50s again across\nmost of the plains.\n\nOur next system of interest is still on track to arrive on Friday.\nAcross the plains, Friday looks mild and breezy in a broad\nsouthwesterly flow regime. Meanwhile, boundary layer moisture\nshould increase across the higher elevations, and should allow \nmountain snow showers to develop as early as Friday morning. The\nfirst of two shortwaves races across the northern half of the\nstate during the day, and aided by a 110kt jet streak... this\ncould produce a band or two of light to moderate snow\naccumulations across the central/northern mountains.\n\nThe second of two shortwaves associated with the large scale\ntrough then pivots through the region Friday night into Saturday.\nThis should provide another round of snow across most of the\nhigher elevations. There's been noteworthy shifts with the \ntiming/orientation of this wave in relation to the northern stream\nvort max in this period, and it seems likely there are still \nseveral shifts to go here. In the meantime, this interim solution \nis a bit wetter across the plains, with a handful of deterministic\nsolutions (and about 30-50% of ensemble members) producing some \nvery light snow accumulation across portions of northern Colorado.\nI would like to see a few more cycles of both physics-based and \nAI ensemble guidance before getting to enthused about precip \nchances along the I-25 corridor.\n\nGuidance throws another weak shortwave towards our forecast area\nsometime early next week, with generally light QPF amounts over\nthe mountains. There's also a good signal that cooler weather will\narrive and stick around through at least the first half of next\nweek, with temperatures near/below normal favored.",
